      "page_idx": 19,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.116,
            0.069,
            0.145,
            0.085
          ],
          "content": "60"
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.184,
            0.068,
            0.489,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Ganita Manjari | Grade 9 | Part I"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.107,
            0.885,
            0.147
          ],
          "content": "Since one digit is non-repeating, we multiply by $1 0 ^ { 1 } = 1 0$ and obtain $1 0 x = 1 . \\overline { { 6 } }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.155,
            0.884,
            0.198
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3:** Now, to move one full repeating cycle (1 digit repeating), we multiply by $1 0 ^ { 1 } = 1 0$ again and get 100x = 16.6."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.2,
            0.884,
            0.223
          ],
          "content": "**Step 4:** Subtracting the first shifted equation from the second, we get,"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.225,
            0.714,
            0.265
          ],
          "content": "$100x - 10x = 16.\\overline{6} - 1.\\overline{6}$ or 90x = 15. Thus, $X = \\frac{15}{90} = \\frac{1}{6}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.268,
            0.615,
            0.301
          ],
          "content": "**Example 8:** Convert 2.357 into the form ${ \\frac { p } { q } } ,$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.302,
            0.378,
            0.324
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** Let x = 2.357."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.328,
            0.885,
            0.392
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** Here, ‘35’ is non-repeating (2 digits), ‘7’ repeats (1 digit). Firstly, we multiply by $1 0 ^ { 2 } = 1 0 0$ to move the non-repeating digits before the decimal and we obtain 100x = 235.7."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.395,
            0.884,
            0.439
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3:** Now, we multiply by $1 0 ^ { 1 } = 1 0$ to move the full repeating cycle and get 1000x = 2357.7."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.442,
            0.801,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "**Step 4:** Subtracting, we obtain $1000x-100x=2357.7-235.7$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.465,
            0.596,
            0.507
          ],
          "content": "Hence, 900x = 2122 and $x = \\frac{2122}{900} = \\frac{1061}{450}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.511,
            0.627,
            0.543
          ],
          "content": "**Example 9:** Convert 2.4537 into the form $\\frac{p}{q}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.545,
            0.39,
            0.567
          ],
          "content": "**Step 1:** Let $x = 2 . 4 5 { \\overline { { 3 7 } } } .$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.575,
            0.884,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": "**Step 2:** Here, $^{ \\circ }45^{\\circ}$ is non-repeating (2 digits), ‘37’ repeats (2 digits). Firstly, we multiply by $1 0 ^ { 2 } = 1 0 0 ^ { \\bar { 1 } }$ to move the non-repeating digits before the decimal and we obtain 100x = 245.37."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.641,
            0.885,
            0.685
          ],
          "content": "**Step 3:** Now, we multiply by $1 0 ^ { 2 }   =   1 0 0$ to move the full repeating cycle and get 10000x = 24537.37."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.691,
            0.85,
            0.713
          ],
          "content": "**Step 4:** Subtracting, we obtain $10000x-100x=24537.\\overline{37}-245.\\overline{37}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.717,
            0.633,
            0.759
          ],
          "content": "Hence, 9900x = 24292 and $\\frac{24292}{9900}=\\frac{6073}{2475}$"
        },
        {
          "type": "table",
          "bbox": [
            0.117,
            0.794,
            0.88,
            0.899
          ],
          "content": "| Decimal type | Steps to follow |\n| --- | --- |\n| Pure repeating | • Let x = the decimal number. • Multiply by 10n where n = the number of repeating digits, • Subtract from the original equation, and solve for x. |",
